WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.269
October 3

2
00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:05.226
Dear Daddy Longlegs,

3
00:00:05.227 --> 00:00:08.228
I'm back at school, and I'm a senior.

4
00:00:08.229 --> 00:00:12.697
This year I'm the editor of the student magazine.

5
00:00:12.698 --> 00:00:17.996
You'll never guess what was waiting for me when I arrived.

6
00:00:17.997 --> 00:00:21.711
A letter from Jervis Pendleton!

7
00:00:21.712 --> 00:00:25.215
He'd sent it to Lock Willow Farm.

8
00:00:25.216 --> 00:00:29.614
But I wasn't there because I was at Sallie's summer house.

9
00:00:29.615 --> 00:00:33.216
Mrs. Semple forwarded the letter to me at college.

10
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:36.384
Here's what Jervie had to say:

11
00:00:36.385 --> 00:00:39.925
"I'm sorry, but I won't be able to get to Lock Willow.

12
00:00:39.926 --> 00:00:43.964
I've accepted an invitation to go yachting with some friends.

13
00:00:43.965 --> 00:00:47.350
I hope you had a nice summer and that you're enjoying the farm."

14
00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:51.726
I don't know what he was trying to do.

15
00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:55.952
He knew my plans had changed and that I wasn't at Lock Willow.

16
00:00:55.953 --> 00:00:59.825
Julia had told him that I was in the mountains with Sallie.

17
00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:02.812
You men should leave scheming to women.

18
00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:05.839
We are much better at it!

19
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.354
I'm living with Sallie and Julia again.

20
00:01:09.355 --> 00:01:13.370
Sallie was the first to arrive.

21
00:01:13.371 --> 00:01:16.873
She helped me unpack my trunk.

22
00:01:16.874 --> 00:01:20.118
My clothes are so stylish now.

23
00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:25.223
It's hard to believe that three years ago I was living in an orphanage.

24
00:01:25.224 --> 00:01:29.366
"You have so many pretty outfits this year."

25
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:35.822
Sallie was admiring a new blue skirt with matching jacket.

26
00:01:35.823 --> 00:01:37.779
"I have a confession.

27
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.440
I copied Mrs. Paterson's wardrobe!"

28
00:01:41.441 --> 00:01:45.275
"How did you manage that?" Sallie asked.

29
00:01:45.276 --> 00:01:49.009
"I found an inexpensive dressmaker this summer."

30
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.323
I pulled a green gown from my trunk.

31
00:01:52.324 --> 00:01:57.830
"This isn't an exact copy of Mrs. Paterson's dress, but I really like it."

32
00:01:57.831 --> 00:02:01.964
"You'll look beautiful in that!" Sallie said.

33
00:02:01.965 --> 00:02:06.455
"Thank you. I'm really pleased with the way my clothes turned out."

34
00:02:06.456 --> 00:02:11.819
And I was really pleased—until Julia arrived.

35
00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.924
Her trunk was full of the most beautiful dresses I've ever seen.

36
00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:21.705
She showed us a rainbow-colored ball gown.

37
00:02:21.706 --> 00:02:26.778
"That looks like something an angel would wear!" Sallie exclaimed.

38
00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:32.153
"I found it in a little shop in France," Julia said.

39
00:02:32.154 --> 00:02:35.373
"There are the most exquisite dress shops in Paris.

40
00:02:35.374 --> 00:02:38.105
And the hat shops!"

41
00:02:38.106 --> 00:02:43.124
She sighed at the memory. "They're wonderful!"

42
00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:47.623
"I must go to Paris someday!" I said.

43
00:02:47.624 --> 00:02:51.882
I suppose you think we're silly to fuss over clothes like this.

44
00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:54.918
But I do love pretty dresses.

45
00:02:54.919 --> 00:03:00.016
Speaking of clothes . . . the maid for our hallway wears a gingham apron.

46
00:03:00.017 --> 00:03:03.598
Just like the dresses I used to wear at the orphanage! I

47
00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:07.774
get a chill every time I see her. I

48
00:03:07.775 --> 00:03:10.756
want to get her some new aprons. Then

49
00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:14.286
I'll sink the blue ones in the lake! Yours

50
00:03:14.287 --> 00:03:15.399
ever, Judy

51
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.296
November

52
00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:19.989
17 Dear

53
00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.372
Daddy Longlegs, I

54
00:03:22.373 --> 00:03:24.826
need some sympathy. My

55
00:03:24.827 --> 00:03:28.853
literary career has had a setback. All

56
00:03:28.854 --> 00:03:32.567
last winter I was writing a novel. This

57
00:03:32.568 --> 00:03:37.801
summer I worked on it whenever I wasn't tutoring my two silly girls. I

58
00:03:37.802 --> 00:03:43.225
finished it right before school started and sent it to a publisher. Well,

59
00:03:43.226 --> 00:03:46.396
he just rejected it. He

60
00:03:46.397 --> 00:03:51.433
noticed my return address and realized that I was still at school. He

61
00:03:51.434 --> 00:03:54.797
suggested I concentrate on my studies. Then

62
00:03:54.798 --> 00:03:58.513
I could try writing again after I graduate! He

63
00:03:58.514 --> 00:04:03.859
criticized my plot, my dialogue, and my sense of humor. And

64
00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.251
I thought I'd written the Great American Novel! I

65
00:04:08.252 --> 00:04:12.411
researched it while I was at Julia's last Christmas. But

66
00:04:12.412 --> 00:04:18.173
I guess two weeks wasn't long enough to capture the spirit of New York City. So

67
00:04:18.174 --> 00:04:21.440
I burned my manuscript. Every

68
00:04:21.441 --> 00:04:24.539
single page! I

69
00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.968
went to bed last night totally dejected. I

70
00:04:27.969 --> 00:04:31.277
felt like I would never become a writer. I

71
00:04:31.278 --> 00:04:36.460
also thought you'd thrown away your money trying to educate me. But

72
00:04:36.461 --> 00:04:41.755
then I woke up this morning with a wonderful new plot in my head! I've

73
00:04:41.756 --> 00:04:44.930
been working on my characters all day. I'm

74
00:04:44.931 --> 00:04:48.257
so happy to be writing again. You

75
00:04:48.258 --> 00:04:52.199
have to admit I'm an optimist. Affectionately,

76
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.520
Judy

77
00:04:53.521 --> 00:04:55.856
December

78
00:04:55.857 --> 00:04:57.344
17 Dear

79
00:04:57.345 --> 00:04:59.793
Daddy Longlegs, I

80
00:04:59.794 --> 00:05:04.377
had the strangest dream last night. I

81
00:05:04.378 --> 00:05:09.351
went into a bookstore, looking for something to read. "Do

82
00:05:09.352 --> 00:05:14.067
you like fiction or nonfiction?" the clerk asked me. "I

83
00:05:14.068 --> 00:05:16.018
like them both. You

84
00:05:16.019 --> 00:05:20.213
have so many books here, I don't know where to start." "Let

85
00:05:20.214 --> 00:05:23.229
me show you our latest bestseller." He

86
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.668
handed me a new book with a red cover. "Oh

87
00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:30.532
my!" I said in surprise. The

88
00:05:30.533 --> 00:05:35.343
book was The Life and Letters of Judy Abbott .

89
00:05:35.344 --> 00:05:40.657
The John Grier Home was pictured on the cover, and my photo was inside.

90
00:05:40.658 --> 00:05:43.914
I bought the book and took it home.

91
00:05:43.915 --> 00:05:46.911
Then I sat up half the night reading.

92
00:05:46.912 --> 00:05:53.332
I was just about to find out my husband's name when I woke up!

93
00:05:53.333 --> 00:05:56.669
By the way, my real book is going well.

94
00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:00.442
I've finished four chapters and drafted five more.

95
00:06:00.443 --> 00:06:05.479
I'll be a great writer yet. Just watch me!

96
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:06.852
Yours affectionately,

97
00:06:06.853 --> 00:06:08.203
Judy

